# For That Day!
Tamil title: அந்த நாளுக்காக!

Many Asian countries assembled. Only after they gathered did the general public even learn the names of many of them. Nations so ordinary as these nevertheless stood proudly at the conference.

China, caught in internal turmoil, proclaimed its greatness with vigour. Tibet, which conducts a religious government, arrived in the manner of a festival procession. Many other countries too sent delegates there.

Dravida Nadu possesses a history older and more glorious than that of every country assembled there. Yet no one thought of it! When many of these Asian countries were still forests, Dravida Nadu maintained commercial relations with the Greek and Roman empires. There was no one there to say so.

In days when many Asian countries had not yet developed their languages fully, the *Tolkappiyam* was composed in Dravida Nadu. No one at the Asian conference spoke about that country’s history or its relations with the world. In districts close to the very place where they assembled, excavations at Mohenjo-daro and Harappa had established the antiquity of Dravidian civilization.

None of the dignitaries assembled there had the inclination or the time to notice these things. Why?

Dravida Nadu lies diminished. It lies as an impoverished country.

Those who have gone from Dravida Nadu to many countries of Asia are labourers who toil and grow weary on tea plantations. Why should countries that live proudly beneath their own flags think about a land that merely sends them coolies?

Dravida Nadu’s present degradation has corrupted the eyes and minds of the Asian countries. An Asian conference conducted in forgetfulness of Dravida Nadu is a caricature in history.

Today that conference followed the path shown by those who possess the glare of publicity. In the future, the Asian countries will recognize their error. They can be made to recognize it if Dravida Nadu obtains the sovereign right to govern itself.

The disregard shown by foreigners is itself the consequence of the divisions that have arisen among us here.

On one side is the unjust attitude of our Andhra friends, who say that Madras cannot be the capital of Tamil Nadu, whose boundaries Sangam literature attests as “from northern Venkata to southern Kumari,” and declare that Madras belongs to them.

On another side is the folly of believing that the support, blessing, patronage, and cooperation of Aryanism are indispensable to Tamil people, Tamil Nadu, the Tamil language, Tamil literature, and Tamil religion alike.

On yet another side is the indifference of Tamils who enter politics believing that forgetting their people is a shortcut to power.

Elsewhere stands the arrogance of authority, which invokes law, peace, and order and uses repression when the poor agitate to rid themselves of poverty.

There is also the humiliating condition created by people who grant Northern leaders the authority, on every matter and at every moment, to mediate, negotiate compromises, conduct inquiries, pronounce judgments, and punish, and who then live bent before them.

Another source of trouble is the class of people who conduct politics as commerce, saying, “We took part in the freedom struggle; therefore we must receive a share in the government and a reward through it.”

Then there is the cruelty of North Indian economic imperialism, which addresses Dravida Nadu thus: “The West will no longer prevail; even if it does, it will govern only in partnership with us. You must therefore submit to our rule of wealth.”

Those who come forward to labour for the liberation of Dravida Nadu, trapped and crushed amid all these forces, meet opposition from every direction.

In this condition, there is nothing surprising in Sultan Shahriar forgetting Dravida Nadu; nor is Nehru’s failure to speak of it impossible to understand.

The youth of Dravida Nadu have marked this day in their seething hearts: the day on which Dravida Nadu, the most ancient of the Asian lands, was forgotten, while faded countries were placed in the glare of publicity.

Today they have not yet reached a position from which they can ask, “Why?” Tomorrow they will ask. They will certainly ask whether a conference of Asian nations conducted in forgetfulness of Dravida Nadu was not a farce.

Not in ancient Delhi; not near the Khyber Pass, which repeatedly gave outsiders a way in; but on the banks of the Kaveri, an Asian conference will one day be held. Even if we are not there, our sons and daughters will assemble at that conference. They will point to Rajendra Chola. The *Silappatikaram* will resound. The distinction of the Chera will be made known. “One community, one God” will be proclaimed. The principle that the Tamil bows at no one’s feet and enslaves no one will be discussed.

Believing that such a day will come, we shall endure the injustice and humiliation inflicted today. Indeed, we have continued to endure them.

13 April 1947

---NOTES---
The article responds to an Asian conference held in 1947 and deliberately contrasts contemporary political visibility with the antiquity claimed for Dravidian civilization. Historical names and political formulations are translated as printed; uncertain OCR readings are recorded separately rather than silently normalized in the Tamil source.
